We'll complete Experiments 9, 10, 11 and 12 from scratch on your Windows PC, using simple commands and step-by-step instructions.

### Tools required

- Windows PC (Host)
- Ubuntu VM in VirtualBox
- Git and GitHub account
- FileZilla
- VS Code
- MySQL
- Vercel account

We'll use:

- Windows: Host machine
- Ubuntu: Virtual machine
- FileZilla: File transfer
- GitHub: Version control and repository hosting

# Experiment 9: FTP File Transfer to VM and GitHub Push

Aim: Transfer files from Windows to Ubuntu VM using FTP, initialize a Git repository and push it to GitHub.

## Step 1. Configure Ubuntu VM network

1. Open VirtualBox.
2. Select your Ubuntu VM.
3. Go to Settings → Network.
4. Set Adapter 1 to Bridged Adapter.
5. Start Ubuntu.

## Step 2. Install FTP server on Ubuntu

Open Ubuntu Terminal:

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install vsftpd -y
```

Start FTP:

```
sudo systemctl start vsftpd
sudo systemctl enable vsftpd
```

Check status:

```
sudo systemctl status vsftpd
```

Press `q` to exit.

## Step 3. Get Ubuntu IP address

```
hostname -I
```

Example output:

```
192.168.1.105
```

Note your actual IP address.

## Step 4. Create a project folder

```
mkdir -p ~/ftp-project
```

Create a sample file:

```
echo "Hello from Ubuntu VM" > ~/ftp-project/index.html
```

## Step 5. Configure FTP

Open configuration:

```
sudo nano /etc/vsftpd.conf
```

Find and ensure these settings are enabled:

```
anonymous_enable=NO
local_enable=YES
write_enable=YES
```

Save using `Ctrl + O`, Enter, then `Ctrl + X`.

Restart FTP:

```
sudo systemctl restart vsftpd
```

## Step 6. Connect using FileZilla on Windows

1. Install FileZilla Client on Windows.
2. Open FileZilla.
3. Enter:

| Field    | Value                |
| -------- | -------------------- |
| Host     | Ubuntu IP address    |
| Username | Your Ubuntu username |
| Password | Your Ubuntu password |
| Port     | 21                   |

4. Click Quickconnect.
5. On the right side, open:

```
/home/your-username/ftp-project
```

6. On the left side, select a file from Windows.
7. Drag it to the right side.

Your file is now transferred to Ubuntu.

## Step 7. Initialize Git repository in Ubuntu

```
cd ~/ftp-project
```

Initialize Git:

```
git init
```

Add files:

```
git add .
```

Commit:

```
git commit -m "Initial commit"
```

Set branch:

```
git branch -M main
```

## Step 8. Create GitHub repository

1. Open [GitHub](https://github.com?utm_source=chatgpt.com).
2. Click New Repository.
3. Repository name: `ftp-project`
4. Select Public.
5. Do not initialize with README.
6. Click Create Repository.

Copy the repository URL.

## Step 9. Push to GitHub

Replace the URL below with your actual repository URL:

```
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/ftp-project.git
```

Push:

```
git push -u origin main
```

If GitHub asks for authentication, complete its browser authentication flow.

## Step 10. Verify

Open your GitHub repository.

You should see:

```
ftp-project/
    index.html
```

Experiment 9 complete

# Experiment 10: Remote Access and Git Synchronization

Aim: Clone a GitHub repository on Windows and Ubuntu VM, synchronize changes using Git and validate using FileZilla.

We will use the GitHub repository created in Experiment 9.

## Step 1. Install Git on Windows

Download Git:

[Download Git for Windows](https://git-scm.com/downloads/win?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Open PowerShell and verify:

```
git --version
```

## Step 2. Clone repository on Windows

Open PowerShell:

```
cd Desktop
```

Clone the repository:

```
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/ftp-project.git
```

Enter the folder:

```
cd ftp-project
```

## Step 3. Make changes on Windows

Open the project:

```
notepad index.html
```

Add:

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Git Synchronization</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Updated from Windows Host</h1>
    <p>Git synchronization experiment.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

Save and close.

## Step 4. Push changes to GitHub

```
git add .
git commit -m "Updated from Windows"
git push origin main
```

## Step 5. Clone repository on Ubuntu VM

Open Ubuntu Terminal:

```
cd ~
```

Clone the same repository:

```
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/ftp-project.git
```

Enter the folder:

```
cd ftp-project
```

Check files:

```
ls
```

## Step 6. Synchronize changes

If you make another change on Windows, push it:

```
git add .
git commit -m "Second update"
git push origin main
```

Now update Ubuntu:

```
cd ~/ftp-project
git pull origin main
```

Verify:

```
cat index.html
```

You should see the latest changes.

## Step 7. Validate using FileZilla

1. Open FileZilla on Windows.
2. Connect to Ubuntu using its IP address.
3. Enter the remote directory:

```
/home/your-username/ftp-project
```

4. Locate `index.html`.
5. Download it to Windows.
6. Open it and verify the updated content.

Experiment 10 complete

# Experiment 11: Split Deployment Website with Form and MySQL Backend

Aim: Create a website with a frontend form on Windows and a PHP/MySQL backend on Ubuntu VM.

We'll use:

- Frontend: HTML, CSS and JavaScript
- Backend: PHP
- Database: MySQL
- Server: Apache

## Step 1. Install Apache, PHP and MySQL on Ubuntu

Open Ubuntu Terminal:

```
sudo apt update
```

Install everything:

```
sudo apt install apache2 mysql-server php libapache2-mod-php php-mysql -y
```

Start services:

```
sudo systemctl start apache2
sudo systemctl start mysql
```

Enable them:

```
sudo systemctl enable apache2
sudo systemctl enable mysql
```

## Step 2. Create MySQL database

Open MySQL:

```
sudo mysql
```

Create database:

```
CREATE DATABASE studentdb;
```

Select database:

```
USE studentdb;
```

Create table:

```
CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100),
    email VARCHAR(100)
);
```

Check table:

```
SHOW TABLES;
```

Exit:

```
EXIT;
```

## Step 3. Create PHP backend

Create a folder:

```
sudo mkdir -p /var/www/html/api
```

Create PHP file:

```
sudo nano /var/www/html/api/submit.php
```

Paste this code:

```
<?php

header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *");
header("Access-Control-Allow-Methods: POST, OPTIONS");
header("Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type");

if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_METHOD"] === "OPTIONS") {
    exit;
}

$conn = new mysqli("localhost", "root", "", "studentdb");

if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Database connection failed");
}

$data = json_decode(file_get_contents("php://input"), true);

$name = $data["name"] ?? "";
$email = $data["email"] ?? "";

$stmt = $conn->prepare(
    "INSERT INTO students (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)"
);

$stmt->bind_param("ss", $name, $email);

if ($stmt->execute()) {
    echo "Student details saved successfully!";
} else {
    echo "Error saving details";
}

$stmt->close();
$conn->close();

?>
```

Save using `Ctrl + O`, Enter, `Ctrl + X`.

This example uses Ubuntu's local MySQL root account through the default socket authentication. Do not expose this development setup publicly.

## Step 4. Check Ubuntu IP

```
hostname -I
```

Example:

```
192.168.1.105
```

## Step 5. Test PHP backend

Open your Windows browser and visit:

```
http://192.168.1.105/api/submit.php
```

Replace the IP with your Ubuntu IP.

You may see a blank page because the backend expects a POST request. That is normal.

If the page cannot load, check Ubuntu's firewall:

```
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
```

## Step 6. Create frontend on Windows

Create a folder on your Desktop:

```
mkdir split-deployment
cd split-deployment
```

Create an HTML file:

```
notepad index.html
```

Paste:

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Student Registration</title>
</head>

<body>

    <h2>Student Registration Form</h2>

    <form id="studentForm">

        <input type="text" id="name"
        placeholder="Enter Name" required>

        <br><br>

        <input type="email" id="email"
        placeholder="Enter Email" required>

        <br><br>

        <button type="submit">Submit</button>

    </form>

    <p id="result"></p>

    <script>

    document.getElementById("studentForm")
    .addEventListener("submit", async function(e) {

        e.preventDefault();

        const name = document.getElementById("name").value;
        const email = document.getElementById("email").value;

        try {

            const response = await fetch(
                "http://192.168.1.105/api/submit.php",
                {
                    method: "POST",
                    headers: {
                        "Content-Type": "application/json"
                    },
                    body: JSON.stringify({name, email})
                }
            );

            const result = await response.text();

            document.getElementById("result").innerText = result;

        } catch (error) {

            document.getElementById("result").innerText =
            "Connection failed";

        }

    });

    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

Important: Replace `192.168.1.105` with your actual Ubuntu IP address.

## Step 7. Run frontend

Open VS Code.

1. Open the `split-deployment` folder.
2. Install the Live Server extension.
3. Right-click `index.html`.
4. Select Open with Live Server.

Your website will open in the browser.

## Step 8. Test the form

Enter:

```
Name: Cliff
Email: cliff@gmail.com
```

Click Submit.

Expected output:

```
Student details saved successfully!
```

## Step 9. Verify MySQL data

On Ubuntu:

```
sudo mysql
```

Run:

```
USE studentdb;

SELECT * FROM students;
```

You should see the submitted student details.

Experiment 11 complete

Important database configuration: Before testing, create a dedicated MySQL user rather than using root.

On Ubuntu:

```
sudo mysql
```

Run:

```
CREATE USER 'studentapp'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'Student@123';

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON studentdb.* TO 'studentapp'@'localhost';

FLUSH PRIVILEGES;

EXIT;
```

In `submit.php`, change the connection line to:

```
$conn = new mysqli("localhost", "studentapp", "Student@123", "studentdb");
```

Save the file. Now test the form again.

# Experiment 12: Build and Deploy Static Website using GitHub and Vercel

Aim: Create a static website, upload it to GitHub and deploy it using Vercel.

## Step 1. Create project folder

Open Windows PowerShell:

```
cd Desktop
mkdir static-website
cd static-website
```

## Step 2. Create HTML file

```
notepad index.html
```

Paste:

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>

    <title>My Static Website</title>

    <style>

        body {
            font-family: Arial;
            text-align: center;
            background: #f0f4f8;
            padding: 50px;
        }

        h1 {
            color: #2563eb;
        }

        button {
            padding: 12px 25px;
            background: blue;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            cursor: pointer;
        }

    </style>

</head>

<body>

    <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>

    <p>This is my first deployed static website.</p>

    <button onclick="alert('Website is working!')">
        Click Me
    </button>

</body>
</html>
```

Save and close.

## Step 3. Test website locally

Open the folder.

Double-click `index.html`.

Your website should open in the browser.

## Step 4. Initialize Git

In PowerShell:

```
git init
```

Add files:

```
git add .
```

Commit:

```
git commit -m "Initial website"
```

Set branch:

```
git branch -M main
```

## Step 5. Create GitHub repository

1. Open [GitHub](https://github.com?utm_source=chatgpt.com).
2. Click New Repository.
3. Repository name: `static-website`.
4. Select Public.
5. Click Create Repository.

Copy your repository URL.

## Step 6. Push project to GitHub

```
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/static-website.git
```

Push:

```
git push -u origin main
```

Replace `USERNAME` with your GitHub username.

## Step 7. Deploy using Vercel

1. Open [Vercel](https://vercel.com?utm_source=chatgpt.com).
2. Sign in using GitHub.
3. Click Add New → Project.
4. Select `static-website`.
5. Click Import.

## Step 8. Configure deployment

Use these settings:

| Setting          | Value       |
| ---------------- | ----------- |
| Framework Preset | Other       |
| Root Directory   | `./`        |
| Build Command    | Leave empty |
| Output Directory | `./`        |
| Install Command  | Leave empty |

Click Deploy.

## Step 9. Verify deployment

After deployment, Vercel provides a website URL similar to:

```
https://static-website.vercel.app
```

Open the URL.

You should see:

- Welcome to My Website
- Description
- Click Me button

## Step 10. Update deployed website

Make a change to `index.html`.

Then run:

```
git add .
git commit -m "Updated website"
git push origin main
```

Vercel automatically detects the GitHub update and deploys the new version.

